╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ parametrized_entangling_layer                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses fixed CZ gates for all entangling interactions, which limits the  │
│                          │ circuit's expressivity since entanglement strength cannot be learned. Given the fairly    │
│                          │ low train accuracy (0.57), the model appears to be underfitting rather than overfitting   │
│                          │ (gap is only 0.07), suggesting we need more expressive power rather than more             │
│                          │ regularization.                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose replacing the fixed CZ gates with parametrized CRZ (controlled-RZ) gates using  │
│                          │ the same allowed qubit pairs. This turns each entangling connection into a trainable      │
│                          │ interaction, allowing the model to learn how strongly each pair of qubits should be       │
│                          │ coupled, rather than using a fixed maximal entangling gate everywhere. This should        │
│                          │ increase the model's capacity to fit the training data while keeping the same circuit     │
│                          │ topology and only adding 12 extra parameters (from 162 to 174), which is a modest         │
│                          │ increase.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is a well known technique in the VQC/QNN literature: using CRZ-style entangling      │
│                          │ layers (instead of fixed CNOT/CZ) generally improves trainability and task-fitting given  │
│                          │ comparable circuit depth, since the coupling strength becomes a free parameter optimized  │
│                          │ during training.                                                                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0253                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5                                                      │
│ temperature              │ 0.5                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 0; deleted: 0; modified: 12;                                                       │
