│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_trainable_entanglement_reduce_redundancy                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The original block used RY -> RZ(pre) -> fixed CZ entangling layer -> RZ(post). The       │
│                          │ pre-CZ RZ layer is largely redundant since RY followed immediately by RZ is just a        │
│                          │ two-parameter single-qubit rotation that gets undone in expressivity terms by the post-CZ │
│                          │ RZ layer anyway (both act before/after a fixed non-parametric entangler). Removing the    │
│                          │ pre-RZ layer frees up parameter budget without losing much expressivity, since the        │
│                          │ RY+post-RZ pair already spans the relevant single-qubit rotation axes needed given a      │
│                          │ subsequent trainable entangling stage.                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To compensate and to genuinely improve entangling expressivity (currently entanglement is │
│                          │ completely non-parametric, limiting the circuit's ability to learn correlations relevant  │
│                          │ to the task), I convert half of the fixed CZ gates into trainable CRZ gates. This gives   │
│                          │ the ansatz the ability to learn the *strength* of entanglement between some qubit pairs   │
│                          │ while keeping the other half as fixed CZ for computational efficiency and to preserve     │
│                          │ some inductive bias/structure.                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Net effect: parameter count drops from 162 (27 params/rep * 6 reps) to 144 (24 params/rep │
│                          │ * 6 reps), and part of that freed budget goes into more expressive trainable              │
│                          │ entanglement. This should help reduce the train-test gap (fewer redundant single-qubit    │
│                          │ params, so less overfitting) while improving expressivity via learnable entangling gates, │
│                          │ hopefully improving validation/test accuracy.                                             │
