╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The 12 CRZ entangling gates are currently issued in the fixed textual order of the        │
│                          │ allowed-pair list, which creates an artificial serial dependency chain on qubit 0 (used   │
│                          │ in 4 consecutive gates) and other qubits, inflating circuit depth even though many of     │
│                          │ these gates act on disjoint qubit pairs and could execute in parallel. I computed a       │
│                          │ proper edge-coloring of the entangling graph formed by the 12 (control,target) pairs:     │
│                          │ node 0 has degree 4 (the max degree in the graph), so at least 4 "layers" are needed, and │
│                          │ a valid 4-coloring exists that groups gates with completely disjoint qubits into the same │
│                          │ layer. Reordering the CRZ gate list to follow this coloring (grouping non-conflicting     │
│                          │ gates back-to-back: {[0,2],[1,3],[4,8],[5,7]}, then {[0,3],[1,8],[2,4],[6,7]}, then       │
│                          │ {[0,7],[2,6],[3,5]}, then {[0,8]}) preserves the exact same gate set, parameter           │
│                          │ names/sharing, and gate count, while giving the ASAP depth-scheduling logic the best      │
│                          │ chance to compact the circuit into 4 dependency layers for this block instead of a long   │
│                          │ serial chain. This is a low-risk, structure-preserving change targeting reduced depth     │
│                          │ (and thus faster/more stable training) without any accuracy trade-off, consistent with    │
│                          │ the parallelization recommendation.                                                       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0999                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
